WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.825
Firmware may contain or access device
identities, keys, credentials,

2
00:00:03.905 --> 00:00:06.510
certificates, pairing material,
manufacturing data,

3
00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:09.637
calibration values, configuration secrets,
or service tokens.

4
00:00:09.717 --> 00:00:12.876
These values often determine whether a
device can identify itself,

5
00:00:12.956 --> 00:00:16.226
join a service, receive updates, or access
protected functionality.

6
00:00:16.306 --> 00:00:19.298
Rust ownership helps make sensitive data
flow easier to review.

7
00:00:19.378 --> 00:00:22.537
A type can show where a value is
introduced, which component owns it,

8
00:00:22.617 --> 00:00:23.559
and how it is passed.

9
00:00:23.639 --> 00:00:26.521
But ownership alone does not protect
storage, erase secrets,

10
00:00:26.601 --> 00:00:29.648
prevent diagnostic leakage, or define
replacement procedures.

11
00:00:29.728 --> 00:00:31.834
Those are product design responsibilities.

12
00:00:31.914 --> 00:00:34.519
Provisioning and secure storage need
explicit design.

13
00:00:34.599 --> 00:00:36.926
Teams should know where secrets enter the
device,

14
00:00:37.006 --> 00:00:39.278
whether hardware-backed storage is
available,

15
00:00:39.358 --> 00:00:42.129
which firmware components can access
sensitive material,

16
00:00:42.209 --> 00:00:44.426
how manufacturing and service tools handle
it,

17
00:00:44.506 --> 00:00:47.277
and what happens if a device needs
credential replacement.

18
00:00:47.357 --> 00:00:49.906
Debug output, logs, crash reports,
diagnostic dumps,

19
00:00:49.986 --> 00:00:52.535
and field-support tools are possible
exposure paths.

20
00:00:52.615 --> 00:00:56.439
They should avoid keys, tokens, private
configuration, sensitive identifiers,

21
00:00:56.519 --> 00:00:59.733
raw buffers that may contain secrets, and
excessive internal state.

22
00:00:59.813 --> 00:01:03.747
Redaction should be designed before
release, not bolted on after a review surprise.

23
00:01:03.827 --> 00:01:07.817
Production firmware should avoid
hard-coded production secrets and should plan for

24
00:01:07.897 --> 00:01:11.333
rotation, replacement, revocation, and
retirement where appropriate.

25
00:01:11.413 --> 00:01:13.574
Device identity is not only a variable in
code;

26
00:01:13.654 --> 00:01:16.536
it is part of an operational lifecycle
shared by engineering,

27
00:01:16.616 --> 00:01:18.500
manufacturing, support, and security.
